{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact T0a head 61dc5d2f85a389c224703202c932e9e7cea7fced on builder/shared-expected-t0a-20261109, tree af81f18b, all 12 artifacts, for request b9f85b9a under promise eb5df2be and review request 96041272. One client module now derives the revisions that an act expects. MCP and the CLI forward their public names to it, and Counter uses its result.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- The move. `packages/client/src/expected.ts` holds MCP's `subjectsOf`, `expectedOf` and `viaLeft` without change, apart from the `act` parameter type. It adds `put` and `ownValue`, copied from shape.ts. It imports only contract types. `shape.ts` and `browser-helpers.ts` replace their copies with one forward each, and the client entry exports the module.\n- Own properties. `subject` reads a rule with `ownValue(act.also, …)` and a field with `ownValue(fields, …)`. `put` writes each key as an own enumerable property. A field or alias named like `__proto__` therefore neither reads from the prototype nor disappears from the output. The CLI's old copy read `act.also[name]` and `fields[rule.by]` directly and assigned `expected[name]`. The CLI now gets MCP's safer behavior. That changes output only for such names.\n- Cycles and `via`. `subject` marks a name null before it resolves a chain, so a cyclic `via` ends at null. A chained `via` resolves through the subject that its `of` names.\n- Counter. The model takes `expectedOf`'s result and keeps both messages. It reports a missing target when a transition has no `on` key, and a missing subject when any `also` name has no key. On main, a `code` rule, a `via` rule or an unresolved rule also reached the missing-subject message. That still holds, because `expectedOf` omits those keys.\n- The callers. Page `data.ts`, the retained editor, the views capture, CLI `claim.ts` and `command-change.ts`, and the repo-in-ai example all reach the same function through their existing imports. No caller passes different arguments.\n- The seals. I recomputed both outside the builder's worktree. `packages/client/src/expected.ts` gives adf24f34… and `packages/cli/src/browser-helpers.ts` gives 68ab88a1…, each equal to its literal.\n- The exceptions. The note names each remaining explicit revision map by file and site, with its reason: captured historical revisions in Lending, and fixtures and scenarios that state their keys on purpose. None holds a second subject resolver.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main f3ddcc06 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 12 paths that its 12 artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes: 8 checked, 0 flagged. No dependency, lockfile, Wrangler, definition, pin, scenario or `docs/mcp-tools.json` change.\n- The generated asset. `apps/room/page/assets.ts` changes because the Page bundle includes the moved helper. Gate 1 at a7af51bb reads `exit 1`, failed only by the stale asset, and stays failed. The one maintained Page build then produced the bytes that the failed owner derived. The build wrapper's exit 2 stays recorded beside the separate metadata audit.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at 9295b11c: \"exit 0\". The reconciliation also records driver and launcher exits of 0. Node 145, Counting 56, Plain 4,031 plus one skip, and Native 1,127 plus two skips. After that gate, only the note and the gate2 manifest change.\n- The browser. GitHub reports Page run 42725479793 as successful at 9295b11c.\n- The catalogue. The focused continuation compared the Lending catalogue and `docs/mcp-tools.json` at main 86179b0c and at 2fc5f712. Both pairs are byte-equal.\n- The evidence. The three manifests verify. Their eight files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The derivation now lives in the portable client package, below MCP and the CLI. It reads only the caller's items. `effectiveFields` and the signing boundary st